  "content": {
    "title": "What bonus did Dusty May get for leading Michigan to national title?",
    "text": "April 6, 2026Updated April 7, 2026, 7:44 a.m. ETAs if winning the NCAA basketball championship in his second season in Ann Arbor, Michigan basketball coach Dusty May has 200,000 reasons to rejoice. May received an additional $200,000 bonus for winning the 2026 national championship game, 69-63, over the Connecticut Huskies at Lucas Oil Stadium in Indianapolis on Monday, April 6.Here's what to know about Dusty May's compensation. Dusty May's contract with MichiganMay agreed to a five-year extension last season and officially signed the contract in July. May's deal features a $4.6 million base salary for this season, set to increase by $250,000 each of the next four seasons and peaks at $5.6 million in the 2029-30 season, according to his contract previously obtained and reported by the Free Press. Dusty May's bonuses in 2026This season, May already earned a $50,000 bonus for winning the Big Ten regular-season title outright, a $50,000 bonus for being named Big Ten coach of the year (an award he got from the media, sharing the honor with the coaches' pick. Nebraska's Fred Hoiberg) and earned another $200,000 for getting the Wolverines to the Final Four for the first time since 2018.Because Michigan won it all, that Final Four bonus will be doubled to $400,000.If May is still coach on April 30, he will be given a $150,000 retention bonus. If May leaves prior to April 30, he owes a $7 million buyout, a figure that drops by $2 million each of the next two seasons. Will Dusty May stay at Michigan?May will not seek other college job opportunities this offseason and is expected to return to U-M for the 2026-27 season and beyond, athletic director Warde Manuel confirmed to the Free Press on Sunday.May was rumored to be a top candidate for the North Carolina job that recently opened in Chapel Hill, but according to Manuel, May is expected to return to Ann Arbor.This is U-M's second national championship (in its ninth Final Four), and first since 1989.Shop Michigan national championship gearFree Press sports writer Tony Garcia contributed to this report.Jalen Williams is a trending reporter at the Detroit Free Press. Contact him at jawilliams1@freepress.com.",
